Neurons were pre-selected by the MICrONS pipeline and identified via unit IDs in 
the session metadata. After aligning metadata to the NWB fluorescence matrix 
(unit IDs are 1-indexed so we subtract 1 to index into the columns of the 
fluorescence array), each neuron belongs to a brain area and depth bin. 

A boolean mask is constructed for each group — for example, all neurons where 
`brain_area == 'V1'` — selecting the corresponding columns of the trial × neuron 
feature matrix. This masked submatrix is then passed to the decoder.

To ensure fair comparisons across groups, all groups are subsampled to a fixed 
global floor — the 25th percentile of the minimum neuron count per group across 
all sessions — with a hard minimum of 20. Groups falling below the floor in a 
given session are skipped entirely rather than decoded with insufficient data.

For each trial, the mean fluorescence across all frames within the stimulus window 
was computed per neuron, yielding a single feature vector (1 × n_neurons) per trial. 
Frame times from the NWB timestamps were used to mask only frames falling within 
`[trial_start, trial_stop)`. This produces a trials × neurons matrix per session

A soft-voting ensemble of Logistic Regression and a linear kernel SVM was used, 
both with balanced class weights to account for any trial imbalance across the 
three movie categories.

For each of 10 bootstraps:
1. A random seed is set so subsampling is reproducible but varied across bootstraps
2. The masked neuron population is randomly subsampled without replacement to the floor N
3. Features are z-scored (StandardScaler fit on the full trial set)
4. 5-fold stratified cross-validation is run — stratified to ensure each fold 
   contains proportional representation of all three movie classes
5. In each fold, both classifiers independently produce class probabilities; 
   these are averaged (soft vote) and the highest probability class is the prediction
6. A second run with shuffled labels is performed identically to estimate chance, 
   confirming that accuracy reflects stimulus-driven signal and not classifier bias
7. The normalized confusion matrix (rows = true class, normalized so each row sums 
   to 1) is computed from cross_val_predict, giving per-class recall on the diagonal

Final reported accuracy, shuffle accuracy, std, and per-class recall are all 
averaged across the 10 bootstraps. Averaging over bootstraps accounts for variance 
introduced by the random neuron subsampling.
